it was here at the sewage plant in east  london where they discovered the polio  virus  a routine inspection for a paralyzing  and fatal disease  last seen in the uk nearly 40 years ago  scientists think the sample they found  was from someone who had received a  so-called live vaccine outside the uk  the vaccine virus does have the  potential to cause paralysis  and if it has mutated and there is  evidence that this vaccine virus is  beginning to mutate but the single  biggest message for individuals is that  they can prevent any risk from that  virus by being fully vaccinated so the  message is to make sure that you and  your family are all up to date for your  routine vaccinations  they expect to find between one and  three samples of polio virus in our  wastewater every year what's different  this time is that the samples they've  found have been several months apart and  are still genetically linked  that suggests community transmission  the bechten sewage plant serves four  million people from chiswick in the west  of the capital to ilford in the east  on average in the uk more than 92  percent of children were vaccinated at  one years old and more than 95 percent  by the age of five  in london though those numbers are below  the national average and could have been  affected by the pandemic we don't want  to panic communities we want them to  understand that the risks are extremely  low but this is a really good moment  just take a moment to check am i are my  children fully up to date with vaccines  if not those vaccines protect us all  from a whole range of communicable  diseases so it's really important that  people go to their gps and make sure  they get their vaccine  they will keep monitoring the wastewater  here and elsewhere in the country at the  same time health workers will be asked  to be vigilant and parents to make sure  their children are up to date with their  vaccinations  the risk is low for now  but  that could change ashish joshi sky news 